{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Brennan1963-ruby-HLH-2L-4L",
  "Source": "E. R. Brennan, \"Multi-Layer All-Dielectric Reflection Filters,\" M.S. thesis (Physics), Polytechnic Institute of Brooklyn (1963), thesis adviser A. Zajac. No doi. - Sec. I p. 1 ('a four layer system with alternate films of zinc sulphide and magnesium fluoride'); Sec. II.A p. 4",
  "Notes": "Final four-layer ruby-laser end reflector: the 3-layer ZnS/MgF2/ZnS stack protected by a half-wave MgF2 overcoat (absentee layer)\nSpecification: Reflectance > 60 % at 694.3 nm with a durable outer layer (ZnS is soft and wears quickly, so a half-wave MgF2 layer is added next to air).\nReported: The half-wave MgF2 overcoat 'leaves the reflectivity at 62 %' at 694.3 nm, normal incidence (p. 4), i.e. the same as the 3-layer stack.\nNotes: The outer MgF2 layer has optical thickness lambda0/2 = 2 QWOT (absentee at 694.3 nm) and is adjacent to air; the thesis lists layers outward from the ruby, the same order as here. Ruby assumed n = 1.76; a recomputation should give the same value as the 3-layer design (~64.8 %, vs. 62 % printed). The two experimental filters were deposited on glass slides (index not given) and measured on a Beckman spectrophotometer; those results (Sec. IV, pp. 26-38) are not in the preview.\nLayers from Sec. I p. 1 ('a four layer system with alternate films of zinc sulphide and magnesium fluoride'); Sec. II.A p. 4; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours R 64.8 % at 694.3 nm; paper R 62.0 % (pp. 2-4). As the 3-layer design: the thesis’s indices give 64.8 %, not 62 %.",
